Attached is a rather large patch file that Dave Caplan and I developed.
We were asked by a customer to create a tool that would allow one to
change a binary policy, on a running system, without the presence of the
policy source files.  This patch is the culmination of that effort.  The
changes are to the selinux/module/checkpolicy and
lsm-2.4/security/selinux/ss directories, as well as some minor policy
directory changes.  We did not address the lsm-2.5 directory, nor the
changes Steve is actively working for checkpolicy in the future...this
patch will have to be reworked into those changes at some point. This
patch was diff'ed from the sourceforge CVS tree as of this morning.

By way of a short summary of what we did, below is an extract from the
readme file included in the patch.  Steve, to help you understand our
changes, I'll privately send you a summary of changes and additions we
made.  I actively solicit additionally testing of these tools as they
ended up more complicated than we expected.  Thanks Frank

-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-

Background and Motivation
-------------------------

In general, if you wanted to maintain small variations for a given 
policy, you would be better served maintaining these variations in the 
policy source files and re-compiling new binary policies with the 
variation you need.  However, there are circumstances where it is 
necessary to be able to change small aspects of a binary policy on a 
running system without the policy source files.  

As an illustration, imagine a mobile computing environment where you 
wished to change access to certain communication devices depending on 
environmental circumstances.  For example, if the device is in a 
docking station, allow access to the ethernet device, but not 
otherwise. Or where a proximity detector is used to determine whether 
an authorized user is at the device, and if so, provide greater access 
(e.g., access to input devices) than if a user is not nearby.  In some 
cases such issues could be handled within the policy itself (always 
preferable), but there are times when you need the ability to 
dynamically change the policy, at run time, without the infrastructure 
available to change the source files and re-compile the policy.

This is the motivation behind the binary patch tools.  These tools 
allow you to specify one or more variations from a base policy, and 
compile the patch into a form that can then be applied to the base 
policy in a running system.  To achieve this goal, a policy patch 
language was developed that allows you to write policy changes and 
compile them (in the context of the base policy) using `checkpatch'. 
Checkpatch is directly analogous to `checkpolicy'. The compiled patch 
can then be applied to the binary policy on the running system using 
`polpatch'. Only polpatch is necessary on the running system, along 
with the binary patch files, in order to change the installed policy.


